Work in the hiyroscript/alva repository and implement the following Practice Ground improvements. Inspect and follow the existing architecture and visual language before changing anything. Do not redesign unrelated systems and do not change Quick Battle behavior.

Existing architecture you should preserve

Practice Ground is implemented primarily through:

js/screens/practice-screen.js
js/game/practice.js
js/game/hud.js
js/ui/fighter-roster.js
js/game/arena.js
js/game/character.js
js/game/combat.js
js/data/practice-map.js
styles.css
tests/practice-ground.test.mjs

Important existing behavior:

Practice Ground currently starts with one P1 fighter and no opponent. PracticeGroundScreen owns its More menu and Change Fighter roster. PracticeSession extends Arena. Arena.fighters[0] is the primary fighter and Arena.fighters[1], when present, automatically becomes the camera’s secondary fighter. The shared CombatSystem already supports melee, projectile, clone, and charged-technique hits against any fighter in fighters.

The shared FighterRoster is already used by Practice Ground’s Change Fighter dialog. Reuse it for CPU selection instead of making another roster implementation.

CombatSystem.events already exposes resolved hit data in this shape:

{ type, attacker, target, damage, projectile, summon, technique }

Use those resolved events as the source of truth for CPU damage indicators. Do not independently recalculate attack damage.

Energy is stored on fighter.combat.energy / maxEnergy and is spent through the existing combat flow. Infinite energy must be a Practice Ground-only rule; do not globally alter Quick Battle’s energy behavior.

1. Move the Practice Ground More button

The Practice Ground three-dot More button currently lives at the upper-right of the HUD.

Move it horizontally to the same center position occupied by Quick Battle’s timer/pause control (.hud-center), but put the More button slightly lower vertically.

The target layout is:

P1 HUD stays on the upper-left.

The More button is centered horizontally at the top of the screen, aligned with where Quick Battle places its timer.

Move it downward by a small responsive amount, roughly 8–14 px depending on viewport size. It should clearly look slightly lower than the Quick Battle timer position without being dramatically displaced.

Do not add a CPU HUD panel on the right just because a practice CPU exists.

Update the Practice menu overlay positioning too. Since the More button is no longer in the upper-right corner, its translucent Practice menu should open underneath the centered More button rather than remaining anchored to the right edge.

Preserve the existing glass styling, hover/active/focus states, aria-expanded, and input behavior.

2. Expand the Practice Ground More menu

The current Practice menu contains:

Change Fighter
Return

Change it to this exact order:

Change Fighter
Enable CPU
Allow infinite energy
Return

The CPU and energy labels are stateful as described below.

Use the existing menuButton() styling and navigation conventions.

Do not add Restart, Help, Settings, Change Stage, or any Quick Battle options.

3. Enable CPU / Change CPU

When no Practice CPU exists, the second button must say:

Enable CPU

Clicking it opens a CPU fighter-selection roster.

After a CPU has been selected and spawned, that same menu button changes to:

Change CPU

Clicking Change CPU opens the same CPU-selection roster and allows the player to replace the current CPU with another fighter.

The CPU selection must not navigate away from Practice Ground.

4. CPU fighter-selection roster

Build a second Practice roster using the existing FighterRoster component.

It must use the same large semi-transparent glass presentation as the existing Change Fighter roster, including the stage remaining visible behind it.

Do not duplicate the roster implementation itself. Instantiate another FighterRoster with its own host and its own unique preview element ID so there are no duplicate DOM IDs.

Suggested heading:

Kicker: Practice Ground
Title: Select CPU when no CPU exists, or Change CPU when one already exists.

The standard Back button must return to the Practice menu without changing anything.

When a CPU already exists, place a second button directly next to the Back button in the CPU roster header:

Disable CPU

This button should only be present/enabled when a CPU currently exists.

Pressing Disable CPU must:

remove the CPU from the active Practice session,
remove all player/CPU opponent references,
remove any stale interaction state tied to that CPU,
close the CPU roster,
return to the Practice menu,
change the menu’s Change CPU label back to Enable CPU,
and leave Practice Ground paused until the player resumes.

Focus should return to Enable CPU after disabling.

When enabling CPU for the first time, there should be no meaningless Disable CPU action.

5. Spawning the Practice CPU

Extend PracticeSession so it can optionally contain:

this.player
this.cpu

The session still begins with:

this.cpu = null

When a fighter is selected from the CPU roster, load its sprites through the existing app.loadCharacter() flow exactly as Change Fighter already does.

Spawn a new Fighter using:

the selected fighter definition,
the selected fighter sprites,
slot p2,
label CPU.

Add a second Practice Ground spawn location for the CPU. Keep the existing P1 spawn unchanged and place the CPU approximately the same distance away that the Quick Battle maps separate their two initial fighters, with the CPU facing toward P1. A second PRACTICE_MAP.spawnPoints entry around 300–340 world units to the right of P1 is appropriate.

Once CPU exists:

player.opponent must reference CPU.
cpu.opponent must reference player.
session.fighters must be [player, cpu].

This is important because the existing Arena/Combat code then automatically gives the player a valid target for clone attacks, projectiles, Sphere Rush, melee attacks, pushboxes, camera framing, etc.

The existing Arena camera should therefore frame P1 and the CPU naturally through its existing primary / secondary behavior. Do not create a separate Practice-only camera implementation.

6. The Practice CPU must be completely non-combatant

The Practice Ground CPU is a training dummy.

It must never voluntarily:

walk,
run,
jump,
drop through platforms,
attack,
throw,
use BA1,
use BA2,
use Special,
charge,
use Defense,
block,
dodge,
or otherwise generate gameplay inputs.

Do NOT directly reuse the current Quick Battle TrainingAIController for this feature. That controller is non-attacking but still follows/repositions around the player.

For Practice Ground, use a truly neutral controller. Since Fighter already falls back to neutral input when there is no controller, using no controller for the Practice CPU is acceptable and preferable if it integrates cleanly.

The CPU should still remain a normal Fighter for simulation purposes. That means it should still:

have hurtboxes,
take actual combat damage,
enter hitstun,
play hurt animations,
receive knockback,
fall and land normally,
collide with the stage,
collide through fighter pushboxes,
be targetable by charged techniques/clones/projectiles,
and automatically face its opponent where existing Fighter behavior does so.

“The CPU does not fight” means it never produces intentional gameplay actions. Do not make it intangible or immune to normal hit reactions.

Do not change Quick Battle’s existing TrainingAIController.

7. Changing the player’s fighter while CPU exists

The existing PracticeSession.setFighter() currently rebuilds fighters as only [player].

Update that behavior so changing the Practice player’s fighter does NOT accidentally remove an enabled CPU.

If CPU exists when P1 changes fighter:

replace only P1,
preserve the existing CPU,
rewire newPlayer.opponent = cpu,
rewire cpu.opponent = newPlayer,
set fighters = [newPlayer, cpu],
and preserve the CPU’s selected fighter.

Existing projectiles/clones/charged-technique cleanup associated with swapping P1 should still be handled safely.

If infinite energy is enabled, the newly selected P1 fighter must immediately inherit the infinite-energy Practice rule.

8. CPU damage-taken indicator

Whenever the Practice CPU receives actual resolved damage, show the amount above the CPU’s head.

The text must:

be red,
start with a minus sign,
show the actual damage received.

Examples:

-6
-15
-2.5

Do not display a plus sign or plain unsigned number.

Use PracticeSession’s this.combat.events after Arena resolves combat. Find events where:

event.target === this.cpu

and:

event.damage > 0

Create a Practice-only floating damage indicator from the actual event.damage.

Do not calculate damage from the attack definition yourself because CombatSystem.applyHit() is already the authoritative location that resolves the final amount.

The indicator must visually follow the CPU rather than being placed at a hard-coded screen coordinate.

Anchor it above the CPU’s current head using the same fighter/world-to-screen information already used by Arena’s fighter markers. It should remain readable as the camera moves.

Give the indicator a short training-game-style lifetime: approximately 0.6–1.0 seconds is appropriate. It can drift upward slightly and fade out.

If multiple hits occur rapidly, do not lose damage events. Either maintain a small collection of simultaneous floating numbers or otherwise ensure each actual hit can be represented.

Format integer damage without an unnecessary decimal. For fractional damage, use a concise value rather than long floating-point noise.

Keep this rendering Practice-specific unless a very small generic Arena hook is clearly cleaner. Do not add damage numbers to Quick Battle.

9. Infinite energy Practice option

Under the CPU menu button add:

Allow infinite energy

Clicking it enables infinite energy for the Practice player’s fighter only.

When enabled, immediately change the button text to:

Revoke infinite energy

Clicking Revoke infinite energy disables the rule and changes the text back to:

Allow infinite energy

This toggle should not close the Practice menu. Keep the menu open and preserve focus on the same button so the text change is immediately visible.

While infinite energy is enabled:

P1’s energy must always be treated as full.
Any charged action should be able to pay its normal energy cost whenever its other gameplay requirements are satisfied.
The HUD energy meter should remain full.
Energy-consuming actions must still execute their normal logic and cooldowns; only the resource limitation is removed.

Keep this rule entirely inside Practice Ground. Do not modify CombatState.spendEnergy() globally in a way that affects Quick Battle.

A clean Practice-specific implementation is to ensure the Practice player’s energy is set to maxEnergy before the fixed simulation step so cost checks always succeed, run the normal Arena update, then restore it to maxEnergy after the step so the HUD never shows a depleted value.

When infinite energy is first enabled, refill P1 immediately.

When it is revoked, stop refilling it. There is no need to artificially deduct energy retroactively.

If P1 changes fighter while infinite energy is enabled, keep the option enabled and make the replacement fighter full immediately.

The CPU is not affected by this setting.

A fresh entry into Practice Ground should start with infinite energy disabled.

10. CPU replacement and cleanup

Implement explicit PracticeSession methods along the lines of:

setCPU(def, sprites)
removeCPU()

The exact names may differ, but keep this responsibility in the session rather than manually editing all fighter state from the screen.

When replacing an existing CPU:

clean up the old CPU safely,
create the replacement at the CPU spawn,
keep the current P1 untouched,
rewire opponent references,
clear stale effects that directly reference the removed CPU where necessary,
reset interpolation/accumulator state if required,
and refit/snap the camera if the canvas already has a view.

When disabling CPU:

end any active player technique that is currently attached to/holding the CPU,
make sure no clone, projectile, bind, technique, or opponent reference retains a broken CPU reference,
set cpu = null,
set player.opponent = null,
set fighters = [player].

After removal the existing solo Practice behavior must work exactly as it does today: attacks with no target safely miss/fall back, clone summons requiring an opponent do not spend energy, and the camera returns to following only P1.

